TITLE:: PlotLib
summary:: Plots for the things SuperCollider does not draw by itself
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Classes/PlotLib, Classes/PltScatter, Classes/PltHistogram, Classes/PltBifurcation, Classes/PltPhase, Classes/PltEvents, Classes/PltMeter, Classes/PltSpectrum

DESCRIPTION::
A small set of link::Classes/UserView:: plots with one palette, one set of axes
and one way of working: bifurcation diagrams, phase portraits, histograms, a live
event scatter, a level meter and a spectrum analyser.

For waveforms and running scopes the stock tools are already good, so PlotLib does
not replace them:
code::
Signal.sineFill(512, [1, 0, 1/3]).plot;   // a wavetable
{ SinOsc.ar(200) }.plot(0.02);            // a moment of a signal
s.scope;                                  // running scope
::

SECTION:: The classes

DEFINITIONLIST::
## link::Classes/PlotLib:: || palette, fonts and the pure helpers: strong::map::,
strong::histogram::, strong::extent::, strong::ticks::, strong::ampDb::.
## link::Classes/PltView:: || the base class: window, grid, frame, ticks, caption.
Subclass it for a new plot.
## link::Classes/PltScatter:: || xs against ys, with an optional marker line.
## link::Classes/PltHistogram:: || counts of a collection of values.
## link::Classes/PltBifurcation:: || sweep a parameter of a one dimensional map
and plot its attractor.
## link::Classes/PltPhase:: || phase portrait of a two dimensional map.
## link::Classes/PltEvents:: || live scatter of events over the last few seconds,
with a histogram of what has been heard.
## link::Classes/PltMeter:: || peak and RMS level per channel, from the server.
## link::Classes/PltSpectrum:: || magnitude spectrum, linear or logarithmic in
frequency.
::

SECTION:: How the classes are shaped

Every plot separates computing from drawing. The maths is a class method returning
plain data, so it can be checked without opening a window, and the instance only
turns data into pixels:

code::
// data only, no GUI
PltBifurcation.points({ |x, r| r * x * (1 - x) }, 3.2, 3.2, 0.5, 1, 2000, 64);

// the same computation, drawn
PltBifurcation({ |x, r| r * x * (1 - x) }, 2.4, 4.0).front;
::

The live views take their frames through a strong::pushFrame:: method, which the
server responder calls but anything else can call too. That is how the meter and
the spectrum are tested without a server, and how they can be driven from a
different analysis.

SECTION:: Examples

code::
// a distribution and the shape it has
PltHistogram({ exprand(0.01, 1.0) } ! 8000, 0, 1, 40, "exprand").front;

// period doubling into chaos, with a marker at one parameter value
PltBifurcation({ |x, r| r * x * (1 - x) }, 2.4, 4.0, 0.5).marker_(3.82).front;

// the Henon attractor
PltPhase({ |x, y| [1 - (1.4 * x * x) + y, 0.3 * x] }, 0.1, 0.1, "Henon").front;

// what a pattern is actually playing
(
p = PltEvents("Pwhite", 8, 150, 1200).front;
q = p.watch(Pbind(\dur, Pwhite(0.08, 0.3), \freq, Pwhite(200, 900))).play;
)
q.stop; p.close;

// listening to the output bus
m = PltMeter(2, 0).front;
x = PltSpectrum(0).front;
::

More in the strong::examples:: folder of the quark, and the tests in
strong::tests:: are runnable with code::sclang tests/test-compute.scd::.
